<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Bhuchar Mori</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1591">1591</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Bhuchar Mori was fought in 1591 near Dhrol in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, between a Mughal army under Mirza Aziz Koka and the Kathiawar chiefs led by the Jam of Nawanagar, who had sheltered the fugitive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Muzaffar Shah III of Gujarat. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> won after heavy losses on both sides. Nawanagar then submitted to Mughal authority, and the battle is remembered as one of the largest fought in Saurashtra.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-bhuchar-mori.jpg" alt="Battle of Bhuchar Mori" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mavdanji Bhimji Ratnu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sketch_of_Battle_of_Bhuchar_Mori_from_Yaduvanshprakash.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Muzaffar Shah III had been a titular ruler while rival nobles managed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> among themselves. Invited by Itimad Khan, the noble holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a>, the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> entered the city without a battle on 18 November 1572 and had taken the sultanate by 1573. Muzaffar escaped from prison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> in 1583, raised troops and for a time held Ahmedabad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vadodara">Vadodara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Bharuch</a>, but was defeated by the governor Mirza Khan in 1584 and fled from place to place until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jam-sataji">Jam Sataji</a> of Nawanagar hid him in the Barda Hills.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> sent his foster brother Mirza Aziz Koka to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1588-89 to capture the fugitive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jam-sataji">Jam Sataji</a> refused to give him up and harassed the Mughal army, cutting its supplies, killing stragglers and carrying off horses and elephants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Mirza Aziz Koka gathered between 8,900 and 9,000 men near Dhrol, facing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a> force of 17,000 to 21,000 that included contingents from Junagadh and Cutch and many clans of the region. Skirmishes and night raids, in which the Kathiawar side was successful in the rainy season, went on for about three months. Mirza Aziz Koka opened peace talks, but Daulat Khan of Junagadh made a secret pact with him, and when battle was joined the Junagadh troops left the field.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jam-sataji">Jam Sataji</a> then left the battlefield to secure his state and family, while his minister Jasa Vajir fought on until evening. His son Kunwar Ajaji, who had been at his wedding feast, came with over 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> warriors and was killed after attacking Mirza Aziz Koka with a spear. Losses were heavy on both sides; Jam Sataji lost 67 relatives, among them his son, nephew and son-in-law. The sources differ on the exact day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Mirza Aziz Koka plundered Nawanagar, and in 1592 he besieged Junagadh, whose garrison surrendered after three months. Muzaffar fled to Okha Mandal and then to Kutch, whose ruler, Rao Bharmalji I, handed him over; on 24 December 1592 he cut his own throat near Dhrol, and the Muzaffarid dynasty ended. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jam-sataji">Jam Sataji</a> returned to Nawanagar in 1593, its affairs managed by a Mughal deputy.</p><p>The memorial site on the plateau has 32 memorials, among them the memorial stones of Ajaji and of his wife Surajkunwarba, who committed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a> on his funeral pyre, and eight tombs of Mughal soldiers. For nearly 250 years the people of the region mourned on Shitla Satam, the festival day on which Ajaji died, and the battle became the subject of folk songs, poems and novels.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Bhuchar Mori” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He took refuge with Jam Sataji of Nawanagar State, whose forces fought the Mughals at the <b>Battle of Bhuchar Mori</b> in 1591.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gujarat-sultanate">Gujarat Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1591 its forces fought the Mughals at <b>Bhuchar Mori</b>, after which the Jams accepted imperial rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/nawanagar-state">Nawanagar State</a></li></ul></section>
